          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            Syntactic
            %em substitution
            of/for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/variables"} variables
            is one of the basic operations in formal mathematics, such as in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+logic"} formal logic
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theories
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structural+rules"} structural rules
          %h2#syntactic_substitution Syntactic substitution
          %p
            Substitution is usually defined as an
            %em operation
            on expressions (such
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strings+%28computer+science%29"} strings
            of letters from an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/alphabet"} alphabet
            and representing
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terms"} terms
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formulas"} formulas
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositions"} propositions
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+types"} dependent types
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/variables"} variables
          %p
            Suppose that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            is an expression in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/context"} context
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/variable"} variable
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \,\colon\, X
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type"} type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t \colon X
            is an expression which has the same
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type"} type
            as
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  P[t/x]
          %p
            the result of
            %strong substituting
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi t
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t
            for all occurrences of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
          %p
            For example, if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            is
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mn 2
                  %mo +
                  %mn 2
                  %mi x
                  %mi y
                  %mo +
                  %mn 3
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x^2 + 2x y + 3
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi t
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t
            is
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi y
                    %mo +
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (y+z)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P[t/x]
            is
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi y
                    %mo +
                    %mi z
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mn 2
                    %mo +
                    %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi y
                    %mo +
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi y
                    %mo +
                    %mn 3
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (y+z)^2 + 2(y+z)y + 3
          %p
          #AsOperationOnSyntax.num_remark
            %h6 Remark
            %p
              %strong (substitution is a meta-operation on syntax)
              In this approach, substitution is an
              = succeed "," do
                %em operation on syntax
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/syntax"} syntax
              itself. In particular, the bracket notation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi t
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [t/x]
              is part of “meta-syntax”, not the syntax in question.
            %p
              That is, the literal string of symbols “
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi t
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P[t/x]
              ” is not itself an expression in the language under consideration, but
              %em denotes
              such an expression, in the same way that “
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                    %mo +
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2+2
              ” is not literally an
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integer"} integer
              but
              %em denotes
              the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/numeral"} numeral
              “
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 4
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 4
              ”.
            %p
              Compare also Rem.
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#TypesettingByActualSubstitution"}
              below.
          %h3#simultaneous_substitution Simultaneous substitution
          %p Substitution for multiple variables does not, in general, commute. That is, the expressions
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi s
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                  %mtext and
                  %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi s
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  P[t/x][s/y]
                  \\qquad\text{and}\qquad
                  P[s/y][t/x]
          %p
            are not in general the same: The former substitutes
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi s
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s
            for occurrences of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi t
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi t
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t
            for occurrences of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi s
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi t
                  %mo ,
                  %mi s
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P[t,s/x,y]
          %p
            to denote the
            %em simultaneous substitution
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi t
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi s
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s
            for
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
            %em neither
            occurrences of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi s
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s
            nor occurrences of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi t
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t
            are substituted for; this is generally not the same as either iterated substitution.
          %h3#avoiding_variable_capture Avoiding variable capture
          %p
            If the language in question contains variable
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bound+variable"} binders
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi t
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t
            contains free variables that are bound in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi t
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            and obtain an expression with the desired meaning.
          %p
            For instance, if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            is
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∃
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo +
                    %mi y
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exists y (x + y = 1)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi t
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t
            is the free variable
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P[t/x]
            would produce
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∃
                    %mi y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi y
                    %mo +
                    %mi y
                    %mo =
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exists y (y + y = 1)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            is true (universally in its free variables) if the variables have type
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℤ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∃
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi y
                  %mo +
                  %mi y
                  %mo =
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exists y (y + y = 1)
            is not. The free variable
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi t
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t
            has been “captured” by the binder
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∃
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \exists y
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
          %p
            We say that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi t
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t
            is
            %strong substitutable
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            if performing a literal textual substitution as above would not result in undesired variable capture. If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi t
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t
            is not substitutable for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            by an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/alpha-equivalent"} alpha-equivalent
            expression in which
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi t
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t
            %em is
            substitutable for
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            = succeed "-equivalence" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi t
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P[t/x]
            = succeed "-conversion" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi t
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t
            substitutable for
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
          %p
            In computer implementations of type theories, however, the issue of variable binding and capture is one of the trickiest things to get right. Performing
            = succeed "-conversions" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/de+Bruijn+indices"} de Bruijn indices
            to represent bound variables.
          %h3#as_an_admissible_type_inference_rule As an admissible type inference rule
          %p
            A general property of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theories"} type theories
            (and other formal mathematics) is that
            = succeed "." do
              %em substitution is an admissible rule
          %p
            Roughly, this means that if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/term"} expression
            of some
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type"} type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P[t/x]
            of substitution (as long as
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi t
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            have the same type). This is generally not a rule “put into” the theory, but rather a property one
            %em proves
            about the theory; type theorists say that substitution is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/admissible+rule"} admissible rule
            rather than a
            = succeed "." do
              %span.newWikiWord
                derivable rule
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/derivable+rule"} ?
          %p
            For instance, in the language of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type+theory"} dependent type theory
            the following
            %em substitution rule
            (one of the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structural+rules"} structural rules
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/admissible+rule"} admissible rule
          #eq:SubstitutionRuleInDependentTypeTheory.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (1)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Sub
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ,
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi P
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi type
                      %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi t
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                      %mo ⊢
                      %mi P
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi t
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %mi type
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Sub
                  \\frac{
                  \\Gamma,\; (x:A) \vdash P \;type
                  \\qquad
                  \\Gamma \vdash (t:A)
                  }{
                  \\Gamma \vdash P[t/x] \;type
                  }
                  \\,.
          %p
            Here “admissibility” means that if there exist derivations of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \vdash (t:A)
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo :
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊢
                    %mi P
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi type
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (x:A) \vdash P \;type
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo ⊢
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi t
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                    %mi type
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma \vdash P[t/x] \;type
            %em part
            of a derivation, rather than being a meta-statement
            %em about
            derivations.
          %p
            The substitution rule is closely related to the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cut+rule"} cut rule
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cut+elimination"} cut elimination
          %p
          #TypesettingByActualSubstitution.num_remark
            %h6 Remark
            %p
              %strong (alternative typesetting of substitution rule)
              %br/
              In view of Remark
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#AsOperationOnSyntax"}
              we may re-express the substitution rule
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:SubstitutionRuleInDependentTypeTheory"} (1)
              by using actual syntactic substitutions instead of the meta-instruction “[t/x]” to perform these, if only we make explicit the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/variable"} variable
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type"} dependency
              of all
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terms"} terms
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Sub
                    %mfrac
                      %mrow
                        %mi γ
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mi Γ
                        %mo ,
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %msub
                          %mi a
                          %mi γ
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %msub
                          %mi A
                          %mi γ
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mo ⊢
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %msub
                          %mi P
                          %mrow
                            %msub
                              %mi a
                              %mi γ
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mi type
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mi γ
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mi Γ
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mo ⊢
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %msub
                          %mi t
                          %mi γ
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mi A
                      %mrow
                        %mi γ
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                        %mi Γ
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mo ⊢
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %msub
                          %mi P
                          %mrow
                            %msub
                              %mi t
                              %mi γ
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                        %mi type
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    Sub
                    \\frac{
                    \\gamma \colon \Gamma
                    ,\;\;
                    a_\gamma \colon A_\gamma
                    \\;\;\;
                    \\vdash
                    \\;\;\;
                    P_{a_\gamma} \;type
                    \\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;
                    \\gamma \colon \Gamma
                    \\;\;\;
                    \\vdash
                    \\;\;\;
                    t_\gamma \colon A
                    }{
                    \\gamma \colon \Gamma
                    \\;\;\;
                    \\vdash
                    \\;\;\;
                    P_{t_\gamma} \;type
                    }
            %center
              %img{:src => "/nlab/files/StructuralTypeInferenceRules-230326b.jpg", :width => "650"}/
            %p
          %h2#explicit_substitution Explicit substitution
          %p An alternative approach to substitution is to make substitution part of the object language rather than the metalanguage. That is, the notation
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P[t/x]
          %p
            is now actually itself a string of the language under consideration. One then needs reduction or equality rules describing the relationship of this string
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P[t/x]
            to the result of actually substituting
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi t
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            as in the usual approach. See
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/explicit+substitution"} explicit substitution
            for more details.
          %h2#CategoricalSemantics Categorical semantics
          %h3#definition Definition
          %p
            In the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} categorical semantics
            of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theory
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Recalling that
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terms"} terms
                are interpreted by
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphisms"} morphisms
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/composition"} composition
                of the relevant morphisms.
            %li
              %p
                Recalling that
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositions"} propositions
                are interpreted by
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subobjects"} subobjects
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi t
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t
                into a proposition
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi P
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
                is interpreted by
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback"} pullback
                or
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/inverse+image"} inverse image
                of the subobject interpreting
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi P
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
                along the morphism interpreting
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi t
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t
            %li
              %p
                Recalling that
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+types"} dependent types
                are interpreted by
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/display+maps"} display maps
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi t
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t
                into a dependent type
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
                is interpreted by
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback"} pullback
                of the display map interpreting
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi B
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
                along the morphism interpreting
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi t
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t
            %li
              %p
                Or else, since
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+types"} dependent types
                are also given by
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classifying+morphisms"} classifying morphisms
                into a
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+of+types"} type of types
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/composition"} composition
                of these classifying morphisms with the given morphism.
          %p
            In the third case, there is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coherence"} coherence
            issue: syntactic substitution in the usual approach is strictly
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associativity"} associative
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+model+of+dependent+types"} categorical model of dependent types
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#CurienGarnerHofmann"} Curien-Garner-Hofmann
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#LumsdaineWarren13"} Lumsdaine-Warren 13
          %h3#examples Examples
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            be a suitable ambient
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            in which we are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} interpreting
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logic"} logic
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+theory"} type theory
          %p
            Suppose
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            are
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/types"} types
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/objects"} objects
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/term"} term
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/function+type"} function type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi X
                  %mo →
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : X \to Y
            is interpreted by a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/morphism"} morphism
          %p
            Now a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proposition"} proposition
            about
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terms"} terms
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type"} type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                  %mo :
                  %mi Y
                  %mo ⊢
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  y : Y \vdash P(y)
          %p
            is interpreted as an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/object"} object
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/slice+category"} slice category
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi 𝒞
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                        %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C}_{/Y}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/truncated+object+of+an+%28infinity%2C1%29-category"} (-1)-truncated
            object if it is to be a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proposition"} proposition
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monomorphism"} monomorphism
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi P
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi Y
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  P
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow
                  \\\
                  Y
                  }
                  \\,.
          %p
            For instance if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒞
                  %mo =
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{C} =
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Set"} Set
            then this is the inclusion of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subset"} subset
            of elements of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            on which
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
            is true. And generally we may write
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi y
                  %mo :
                  %mi Y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi isInhab
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  P = \{y : Y | isInhab(P(y)) \}
                  \\,.
          %p
            Now finally the
            %strong substitution
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(x)
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi P
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi P
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi f
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  P(f(-))
                  \\\
                  \\downarrow
                  \\\
                  X
                  }
          %p
            is interpreted as the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback"} pullback
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi P
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi f
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo ≔
                        %mtd
                          %msup
                            %mi f
                            %mo *
                          %mi P
                        %mtd
                          %mo →
                        %mtd
                          %mi P
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mi X
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mi f
                        %mtd
                          %mi Y
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  P(f(-)) \coloneqq &amp; f^* P &amp;\to&amp; P
                  \\\
                  \&amp; \downarrow &amp;&amp; \downarrow
                  \\\
                  \&amp; X &amp;\stackrel{f}{\to}&amp; Y
                  }
                  \\,.
          %p
            Notice that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monomorphisms"} monomorphisms
            are preserved by pullback, so that this is indeed again the correct interpretation of a proposition.
          %p
            Specifically, if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unit"} unit
            type it is interpreted as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+object"} terminal object
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝒞
